From 9e8dd4fed75340839159500b46e5ea2098dc7351 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hongbailingmeng <3471972616@qq.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 11:20:19 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(a2a): create the session under the A2A context id

AgentExecutor looked a session up by the request's contextId, but created it
with createSession(appName, userId), the overload that lets the service
generate the id. InMemorySessionService generates a UUID when no id is given,
so the stored session never matched the id the next turn asked for: every turn
found nothing, created another session, and the agent started from an empty
history.

The A2A context id is the anchor a client sends back on a follow-up message,
so it is now passed as the session id on creation as well. A request without a
context id still gets a generated one, because RequestContext assigns a UUID
before AgentExecutor sees the request.

Using a caller-chosen id makes two first turns with the same new context id
collide, which they could not do when each turn invented its own UUID. A
service that rejects duplicate ids (InMemorySessionService's documented next
default) would fail the losing request, and that peer would receive a failed
task. createSession therefore falls back to reading the session the winner
created, and only for "already exists" -- any other failure still surfaces.

This mirrors adk-python, where the request converter sets
session_id=request.context_id and the executor passes it on to create_session.

Covered at two levels. ContextIdSessionReuseTest drives the executor directly:
two turns sharing a context id land in one session, the second turn sees the
first turn's events, a turn that loses the creation race joins the winner's
session, and an unrelated creation failure still fails the turn.
ContextIdEndToEndTest goes through DefaultRequestHandler.onMessageSend, so the
request context is built by the A2A SDK from MessageSendParams the way a real
client reaches it.
